A||||5||ThatRickGuy||The Thunking Continues/sliding a-arm||||8||
Z||000000||ThatRickGuy||07-18-2001||09:36 PM||||Hey everyone. well, good news is I limped the fiero back to WI with a uhaul trailer  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> other good news is I managed to get the sleeves for the lower control arms and get them installed (Thanks to Bob out at KHF!) bad news is I'm still getting the "thunk" when I turn the wheel just right. I found the culpret, but I can't figure out how to stop it. It's the driver's side upper control arm shaft's rear bolt (ugg, mouth full!). This is an '88 just to make sure. but anyways, this bolt slides the whole length of it's slot when the wheel is turned accross a specific point, resulting in a loud 'thunk' and wheel jerk. I put a ratchet w/ an extra 1 1/2' of cheater bar on it and I couldn't tighten it at all. any idea on how to keep this bolt from moving?? Thanks!!!||||reg||8
Z||000001||Raydar||07-18-2001||10:23 PM||||Any chance it's cross-threaded?<BR>You may have to replace the plate that holds the two bolts (nuts?)<BR>I've had mine partially apart, but it's been a while. Don't remember exactly.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||ThatRickGuy||07-18-2001||10:43 PM||||I'm not sure, it looks tight, metal on metal, and I don't remember having any problems putting it on in the first place. in fact, now that I think about it, it was fine until I got it alligned last... that's when this whole thunking started. hmmm... the bolt wouldn't have been removed during an alignment (I know, I watched the guy work) arg... I look into the cross thread tho. any other ideas? is it possible to install the shaft upside down? w/ the scuffed portion up? hmmm thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||07-19-2001||12:05 AM||||The alignment shop wouldn't have removed these bolts, but they would have loosened them to make the proper adjustments.  I have no idea how they could have cross threaded them, but if you need new bolts and nutplates LMK......I have extras lying around.<P>BTW.....you can attach the shaft "upside down".  Look at the portion of the shaft between the bushings (the only part you can see)......there should be some letters & numbers stamped into it if it is postioned properly.  If you don't see any numbers then it might be "upside down" with the grooves pointing up instead of down.  <P>LMK what you find out.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fierowrecker||07-19-2001||12:07 AM||||Hey Rick!<BR>  I had an alignment guy pull the same crap with me...<BR>Took mine in for alignment, 15 minutes later, my Fiero is junk...<BR>I told the basturd to tighten the bolts back up and I was going to report him...<BR>Normally, I go to a privatly owned alignment shop (Accurate Alignment, shameless plug for a good shop!), but thought I would try a chain store, bad news... But now they are out of business!!!<BR>Ya, I have to pay a bit more at Accurate, but I can trust the job is done properly to begin with...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000005||ThatRickGuy||07-19-2001||03:07 AM||||Mr. Fiero, I'll drop ya a line tomorrow once I find out, it was too dark to make much out when I was out there.<P>Wrecker, this was a privatly owned shop, the guy has owned the place for the last 20+ years. He came highly recomended.<P>The only thing I can figure is that either A) the groves got scraped off, or B) the shaft is upside down, which would be odd because I haven't removed it since january, and this all started in late may/early june. ahh well, thanks for the ideas, I'll post and update tomorrow.||||reg||1
